    {
      "v": 14,
      "zh": "就叫了家里的人来，对他们说：「你们看！他带了一个希伯来人进入我们家里，要戏弄我们。他到我这里来，要与我同寝，我就大声喊叫。",
      "en": "she called for her household servants and said to them, “See, my husband brought[NOTE:1] in a Hebrew man[NOTE:2] to us to humiliate us.[NOTE:3] He tried to go to bed with me,[NOTE:4] but I screamed loudly.[NOTE:5]",
      "notes": [
        "The verb has no expressed subject, and so it could be treated as a passive (“a Hebrew man was brought in”; cf. NIV). But it is clear from the context that her husband brought Joseph into the household, so Potiphar is the apparent referent here. Thus the translation supplies “my husband” as the referent of the unspecified pronominal subject of the verb (cf. NEB, NRSV).",
        "A Hebrew man. Potiphar’s wife raises the ethnic issue when talking to her servants about what their boss had done.",
        "Heb “to make fun of us.” The verb translated “to humiliate us” here means to hold something up for ridicule, or to toy with something harmfully. Attempted rape would be such an activity, for it would hold the victim in contempt.",
        "Heb “He approached me to lie down with me.” Both expressions can be a euphemism for sexual relations. See the note at 2 Sam 12:24.",
        "Heb “and I cried out with a loud voice.”"
